WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.540
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:24.590
Oh, Angela.

9
00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:25.570
Hi there.

10
00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:27.170
We need to go on a search.

11
00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:28.510
We sure do.

12
00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:32.050
For Michael Scott, who is just wandering around Scranton today.

13
00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:33.810
Yeah, he's going on a walkabout.

14
00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:36.910
It is Season 7, Episode 15.

15
00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:38.110
It's called The Search.

16
00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:42.910
It was written by Brent Forrester and directed by a new director, Michael Spiller.

17
00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:44.090
So nice.

18
00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:45.230
So nice.

19
00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:46.570
Michael is the lovelieSt.

20
00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:51.790
Well, you know, he was a cinematographer and a director, which made him perfect for our show.

21
00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:53.790
Here's something that I didn't know, though.

22
00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:57.830
He was the cinematographer for the first four seasons of Sex and the City.

23
00:00:58.570 --> 00:00:59.050
Oh.

24
00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:02.490
Why didn't I know that so I could pepper him with questions?

25
00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:03.710
Oh, I would have had so many questions.

26
00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:04.270
Right?

27
00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:06.110
This is before we Googled.

28
00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:07.150
I know.

29
00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:09.410
It wasn't before Google.

30
00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:12.730
Google was there, but it's before you and I Googled.

31
00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:14.570
We were not ladies who Google yet.

32
00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:15.010
Mm-mm.

33
00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:21.210
Michael also won an Emmy for Outstanding Direction for a Comedy Series for Modern Family.

34
00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:22.910
So we were in very good hands.

35
00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:24.310
How about a summary?

36
00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:24.850
Yep.

37
00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:30.750
When Jim leaves Michael at a gas station without his phone or wallet, Michael goes on a walkabout

38
00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:31.750
around Scranton.

39
00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:35.290
Meanwhile, Dwight, Aaron, and Holly set out to find him.

40
00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:42.510
Back at the office, Pam's artwork on the office fridge inspires some silly captions from her

41
00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:46.610
co-workers, resulting in a caption contest at Gabe's expense.

42
00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:49.630
Are you ready for fast fact number one?

43
00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:50.650
I am.

44
00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:54.490
Well, this one is coming at you straight from Brent Forrester.

45
00:01:54.810 --> 00:02:00.990
Brent is awesome, and his enthusiasm about writing these episodes is always such a delight.

46
00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:02.250
I love hearing from him.

47
00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:02.810
I know.

48
00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:04.490
He loves writing in general.

49
00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:05.610
He loves storytelling.

50
00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:10.770
Well, he sent in an audio clip about how this episode is the perfect rom-com.

51
00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:18.930
I got so lucky when I was assigned to write this episode because it's the climax of the

52
00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:23.630
Michael Hawley romantic subplot that's been running through the show for years.

53
00:02:23.870 --> 00:02:27.930
And the episode itself is structured like a classic rom-com.

54
00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:34.170
Hawley denies that they're made for each other, but each beat of the story seems to prove that

55
00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:35.610
they are made for each other.

56
00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:41.970
And then they meet in this classic romantic comedy trope, the meeting on the rooftop of

57
00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:45.950
the Empire State Building from An Affair to Remember and Sleepless in Seattle.

58
00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:50.210
But because it's the office, it's not a glamorous art deco setting.

59
00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:53.090
It's this seedy rooftop in Scranton.

60
00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:57.150
All of that story material was just handed to me.

61
00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:01.450
Greg and the writing staff came up with it, and I was assigned to write it.

62
00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:04.170
And I get to say, written by Brent Forrester.

63
00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:08.090
It's funny.

64
00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:13.670
When I saw that rooftop kissing scene at the end of this episode, I had the thought,

65
00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:16.390
why did they pick such an ugly roof?

66
00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:18.890
And it was clearly intentional.

67
00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:26.210
They're like, this is the office version of the rom-com Empire State Building.

68
00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:28.890
I recently re-watched Sleepless in Seattle.

69
00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:29.890
It's so charming.

70
00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:31.010
It totally holds up.

71
00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:33.450
I don't remember that one.

72
00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:36.270
I think I'm mixing it up in my mind.

73
00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:37.190
Tom Yanks and Meg Ryan.

74
00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:38.790
I thought that was You Got Mail.

75
00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:40.250
Well, they did that later.

76
00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:41.290
They did that later.

77
00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:44.030
But Sleepless in Seattle is so sweet.

78
00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:45.970
You know, oh my gosh, you have to watch it.

79
00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:47.030
Maybe we should watch it together.

80
00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:47.830
Ooh.

81
00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:50.230
Maybe we should watch it and break it down.

82
00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:50.770
Oh.

83
00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:51.870
Okay.

84
00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:52.490
All right.

85
00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:53.310
Put a pin in that.

86
00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:53.670
Okay.

87
00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:59.930
You know, Brent also shared with us the caption contest storyline in this episode.

88
00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:04.990
He said that idea came because Justin Spitzer, one of our other writers,

89
00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:08.750
had submitted a caption to the New Yorker cartoon conteSt.

90
00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:09.350
Uh-huh.

91
00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:15.070
And they kept waiting to see if his caption was going to get picked, but he didn't win.

92
00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:19.030
And so they got to talking about it, joking about it, and they thought,

93
00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:20.950
that seems like something Oscar would do.

94
00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:23.510
Oscar would submit a caption for a conteSt.

95
00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:26.930
But that eventually grew into this B story.

96
00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:33.250
And Brent said he thought it was perfect because every character was going to get at least one

97
00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:37.310
memorable moment because we were going to get to hear their caption or hear their thoughts on the

98
00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:37.690
conteSt.

99
00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:38.930
And it really worked.

100
00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:41.530
He said it was a great Group B story storyline.

101
00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:47.610
Fast fact number two, you might notice, Jim is not around much in this episode.

102
00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:48.630
I noticed.

103
00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:51.910
He was still off in Alaska shooting Big Miracle.

104
00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:54.450
You know, it was directed by Ken Kwapis.

105
00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:55.930
We mentioned that last week.

106
00:04:56.650 --> 00:05:01.830
I got curious about the film because I'm like, John's been gone now for like three episodes.

107
00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:02.230
Yeah.

108
00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:02.950
What's he doing?

109
00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:04.210
What's he up there doing?

110
00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:11.090
I remembered that the movie was based on a true story of three California whales that

111
00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:15.590
got trapped in the ice in Alaska, and then everybody banded together to save the whales.

112
00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:17.710
But I found an audio clip.

113
00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:24.730
So he starred in this movie with Ted Danson, Drew Barrymore, Kristen Bell, and Ken Kwapis

114
00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:26.410
starts out the audio clip.

115
00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:27.150
Here it is.

116
00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:33.510
In the northernmost town in our hemisphere, off the coast of Alaska, three California gray

117
00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:35.770
whales were discovered trapped in the ice.

118
00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:36.910
You've got to see this.

119
00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:42.990
Three gray whales are now trapped in walls of ice six inches thick.

120
00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:46.310
You have whales in trouble and you didn't call me?

121
00:05:46.390 --> 00:05:48.570
I didn't call you because there's nothing you can do.

122
00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:50.710
There's always something you can do.

123
00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:53.750
NBC put it on national news that evening.

124
00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:58.750
Within 24 hours, every news service sent reporters to Alaska.

125
00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:03.250
There were over 150 journalists that flew to Barrow to report this story.

126
00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:06.710
This media circus sort of grew on the ice.

127
00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:09.070
It captured the imagination of the world.

128
00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:15.330
And so everybody comes to the table to start an effort and a movement to save these whales.

129
00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:20.590
The Inupia people, oil companies, the United States military.

130
00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:21.910
You ready to do this?

131
00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:24.090
Russia, Greenpeace activists.

132
00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:27.010
Why won't you give us a chance to get them out?

133
00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:29.090
Everyone is doing it for their own reasons.

134
00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:32.760
But that's not always bad.

135
00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:36.260
We are all more similar than we're dissimilar.

136
00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:39.200
The right cause at the right time will always win out.

137
00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:41.260
There's so much like us.

138
00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:48.520
We get scared and we're vulnerable and we need help sometimes too.

139
00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:51.860
It is a really beautiful story.

140
00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:54.060
Okay.

141
00:06:54.360 --> 00:07:01.380
Thank you for listening to that whole clip of them promoting this movie because after I found it, I was crying.

142
00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:05.380
I was like, yes, the world needs this.

143
00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:06.580
This is so wonderful.

144
00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:07.900
Everyone came together.

145
00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:10.000
This movie got great reviews.

146
00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:11.880
It has a great tomato rating.

147
00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:13.200
It is a family film.

148
00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:14.840
I'm going to watch this with my family.

149
00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:15.780
I'll report back.

150
00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:18.260
Are you ready for fast fact number three?

151
00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:19.160
I am.

152
00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:22.780
This one is a fan question from Jessica N.

153
00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:24.220
in Rochester, New York.

154
00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:30.020
Jessica would like to know, where did you film the Michael Lost and Wandering scenes for this episode?

155
00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:32.380
Was it all in one area?

156
00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:34.020
Were there multiple locations?

157
00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:40.580
They did a really good job of stitching it all together so it looked like the same place in the same time of day.

158
00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:45.140
Well, Jessica, I checked in with Randy Cordray on this.

159
00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:47.180
And guess what?

160
00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:50.480
This episode was a, quote, logistical nightmare.

161
00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:51.640
I bet.

162
00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:53.400
They went to so many locations.

163
00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:56.200
And they would have to move everybody every time.

164
00:07:56.660 --> 00:07:56.960
Yeah.

165
00:07:57.280 --> 00:07:59.920
There were 10 different locations for this episode.

166
00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:07.940
That's including actual restaurants and shops, but also just cars and shots on the street of Michael wandering.

167
00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:13.800
We filmed this not only over multiple days, but over multiple weeks.

168
00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:16.820
In order to clear John for his movie,

169
00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:25.760
we actually shot the scenes of him and Steve driving and then the scenes of them at the gas station the previous week when we were shooting the seminar.

170
00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:28.580
So we had to match that.

171
00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:35.580
Then a week later, over multiple days, nothing was within walking distance from one another.

172
00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:37.080
That's a real gamble.

173
00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:38.500
Like, think about it.

174
00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:43.040
What if the day they shot with Steve and John the week before was super sunny?

175
00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:48.180
And then what if we got, like, some weird weather, like, surge and it was all overcast and stuff?

176
00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:48.360
Yeah.

177
00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:49.200
I know.

178
00:08:49.340 --> 00:08:52.240
Or if it had rained, which never happens, but could happen.

179
00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:52.720
Could happen.

180
00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:57.660
And in fact, the minute you schedule something like this in film or television, this happens.

181
00:08:58.040 --> 00:08:59.920
Randy must have been so stressed out.

182
00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:03.000
He had a big thank you.

183
00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:10.620
He said, quote, this episode truly showed the expertise of our beloved location manager, Kyle Alexander, and his team.

184
00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:20.420
Randy said, I have raved about Kyle in the past, but this particular episode was a massive challenge, one that he delivered with perfection.

185
00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:31.460
He said, Kyle not only had to find suitable spaces to satisfy the needs of each scene creatively, but he also had to consider the entire shooting company in terms of parking,

186
00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:35.540
base camps, trailers, generators, proximity.

187
00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:39.320
Like, we moved a couple times, but not too many times.

188
00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:41.560
He said he had a very small team.

189
00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:44.740
It was just himself, Christian Krieger, and Anna Coates.

190
00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:46.100
Just three people.

191
00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:51.260
Randy went on to say that they were one of his favorite departments to work with.

192
00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:54.180
They were always drama-free, which is good.

193
00:09:54.800 --> 00:10:02.320
And he says, as I always say about putting together a crew for a comedy, quote, the last thing I want to see is drama.

194
00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:13.360
So I will share more about each of the individual locations as we break them down, but we were everywhere from North Hollywood, Studio City, all the way to downtown LA.

195
00:10:13.780 --> 00:10:14.540
I can't wait.

196
00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:16.460
I love your location breakdowns, lady.

197
00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:18.900
Well, I got some fun little tidbits about them, too.

198
00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:20.080
Okay, I love a tidbit.

199
00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:26.800
But before we go to break, Angela, I got this fan question in the fan mail inbox.

200
00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:30.680
I don't know where or when to ask you this.

201
00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:31.400
Oh, my gosh.

202
00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:33.180
But it is a question for you.

203
00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:33.660
What?

204
00:10:33.860 --> 00:10:35.100
So I thought I'd throw it in.

205
00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:35.540
Okay.

206
00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:37.420
It's from Shannon S.

207
00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:38.620
in Grand Haven, Michigan.

208
00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:42.220
I watched a New Girl episode about Jess's birthday.

209
00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:48.980
In that episode, Angela guest starred, and as she was speaking to Zoe's character, she called her lady.

210
00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:53.400
Angela, was that scripted or just you?

211
00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:54.720
Just me.

212
00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:55.280
Yay!

213
00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:56.060
Yay!

214
00:10:56.740 --> 00:10:58.060
New Girl was so much fun.

215
00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:03.960
I know I've talked about it before, but it really felt like our set and the way that we got everything as it was written.

216
00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:07.320
And sometimes we could play around a little bit and some improvs would make it in.

217
00:11:07.780 --> 00:11:11.060
But that was a fantastic and fun show to be a part of.

218
00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:14.700
And those guys are doing a podcast about that show now, too, right?

219
00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:15.420
Yeah, they sure are.

220
00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:17.380
Hannah and Zoe and Lamorne.

221
00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:19.040
It's called Welcome to Our Show.

222
00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:22.180
Well, Shannon, you might want to check it out as a New Girl fan.

223
00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:24.620
I'll have to go on it when they do my episode.

224
00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:25.260
You will.

225
00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:26.540
Call her lady.

226
00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:27.500
Again.

227
00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:30.700
I have one little tidbit before we go to break.

228
00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:34.960
The original title for this episode was LoSt.

229
00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:36.040
Oh.

230
00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:38.200
Not the search, but LoSt.

231
00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:40.820
I think I would have found that confusing.

232
00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:42.960
That makes me think of Lost, the TV show.

233
00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:43.940
Oh.

234
00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:44.940
I would be.

235
00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:45.920
I don't know.

236
00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:48.020
I'm glad they changed it.

237
00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:48.560
Me too.

238
00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:51.780
I feel like the search is a little bit more positive sounding than LoSt.

239
00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:54.320
Also, he's on a search.

240
00:11:54.500 --> 00:11:54.900
Yes.

241
00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:56.460
It's layered.

242
00:11:56.740 --> 00:11:57.140
Yes.

243
00:11:57.380 --> 00:11:58.520
A search for love.

244
00:11:58.920 --> 00:11:59.320
Really.

245
00:11:59.540 --> 00:11:59.780
Yeah.

246
00:11:59.780 --> 00:12:00.140
In the end.

247
00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:01.140
All right.

248
00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:01.880
Well, let's take a break.

249
00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:02.540
We'll be right back.

250
00:12:11.380 --> 00:12:13.760
Kelly and Ryan are going to make a very important announcement.

251
00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:16.000
They got divorced over the weekend.

252
00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:17.180
Mm-hmm.

253
00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:19.380
And the whole bullpen is like, wait, what?

