508
00:23:17.170 --> 00:23:20.810
There is a Bennett Street in Scranton, but I could not find a gas station.

509
00:23:22.030 --> 00:23:22.430
Yeah.

510
00:23:22.950 --> 00:23:23.990
Pam says, don't worry.

511
00:23:24.150 --> 00:23:27.850
She's going to put out an APB and ask Pam Beasley.

512
00:23:28.550 --> 00:23:31.090
And then she's like, nothing like Jim is silent.

513
00:23:31.310 --> 00:23:32.250
She's like, did you not hear me?

514
00:23:32.350 --> 00:23:33.050
He goes, no, I heard you.

515
00:23:33.050 --> 00:23:35.750
I was like, so harsh.

516
00:23:36.270 --> 00:23:38.870
Pam had some candy bag alts for this line.

517
00:23:39.030 --> 00:23:39.930
You know she did.

518
00:23:40.130 --> 00:23:40.770
Yeah, you did.

519
00:23:40.770 --> 00:23:41.910
Just for this APB?

520
00:23:42.150 --> 00:23:42.930
That's right.

521
00:23:43.090 --> 00:23:43.330
Okay.

522
00:23:43.590 --> 00:23:43.950
All right.

523
00:23:44.170 --> 00:23:45.430
What else did I say?

524
00:23:45.610 --> 00:23:47.510
You said, got it.

525
00:23:47.570 --> 00:23:50.690
I will pass that information on to someone who cares even more than me.

526
00:23:51.050 --> 00:23:53.250
Pam says, here's another one.

527
00:23:53.390 --> 00:23:53.850
Got it.

528
00:23:53.950 --> 00:23:57.770
Give me the address and I will organize a search party of people that are not me.

529
00:23:58.210 --> 00:23:58.690
Okay.

530
00:23:59.610 --> 00:24:00.970
And here's another one.

531
00:24:01.230 --> 00:24:04.390
Aw, speaking of babies in crisis, what's the address?

532
00:24:04.570 --> 00:24:05.690
I'll form a search party.

533
00:24:05.970 --> 00:24:06.450
Okay.

534
00:24:06.890 --> 00:24:08.530
Which I don't think Pam would have said.

535
00:24:08.710 --> 00:24:08.930
No.

536
00:24:09.330 --> 00:24:10.090
You know what?

537
00:24:10.170 --> 00:24:12.670
That brings up remnants of the titae.

538
00:24:12.850 --> 00:24:13.590
The titae.

539
00:24:13.650 --> 00:24:16.050
The titae from the birthing episode.

540
00:24:16.870 --> 00:24:21.630
And then the last one was, I'm sure he's smart enough to find a way.

541
00:24:21.950 --> 00:24:23.010
Actually, you know what?

542
00:24:23.010 --> 00:24:23.930
You better give me the address.

543
00:24:24.310 --> 00:24:25.530
I like that line.

544
00:24:26.670 --> 00:24:33.550
Speaking of the gas station, the employee who works there, who was played by Eugene Cordero,

545
00:24:33.790 --> 00:24:38.830
who's been in everything, The Good Place, Crazy Ex-Girlfriend, Veep, Brooklyn Nine-Nine,

546
00:24:39.310 --> 00:24:41.570
he will not let Michael use the phone.

547
00:24:42.190 --> 00:24:42.490
No.

548
00:24:42.850 --> 00:24:43.150
Mm-mm.

549
00:24:43.350 --> 00:24:45.290
But then Michael will not give up.

550
00:24:45.370 --> 00:24:47.190
So he's like, fine, you can make a call.

551
00:24:47.330 --> 00:24:52.190
But then Michael doesn't know the number for the office anyway because he can't use his speed dial.

552
00:24:52.310 --> 00:24:53.510
He only has it on speed dial.

553
00:24:53.510 --> 00:24:55.330
I mean, how many phone numbers do you know?

554
00:24:55.470 --> 00:24:56.890
Because I feel like this is me.

555
00:24:57.090 --> 00:24:59.010
I know, like, my parents' phone number.

556
00:24:59.510 --> 00:25:02.410
I know, obviously, like, my husband's phone number.

557
00:25:02.490 --> 00:25:03.770
Jenna, I don't think I know your phone number.

558
00:25:04.250 --> 00:25:08.110
I have a phone number in my head, and I don't know if it's yours or my sister's.

559
00:25:08.150 --> 00:25:09.070
I do know it.

560
00:25:09.190 --> 00:25:09.510
You do?

561
00:25:09.650 --> 00:25:09.950
I can see it.

562
00:25:10.170 --> 00:25:10.510
Okay.

563
00:25:10.810 --> 00:25:13.070
But yeah, I know, like, four phone numbers, I think.

564
00:25:13.250 --> 00:25:14.390
Is yours ***?

565
00:25:15.920 --> 00:25:16.240
No.

566
00:25:16.520 --> 00:25:17.340
Whose number is that?

567
00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:17.980
I don't know.

568
00:25:18.720 --> 00:25:19.080
Wait.

569
00:25:19.420 --> 00:25:20.780
Is yours ***?

570
00:25:21.480 --> 00:25:21.840
Yes!

571
00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:22.240
Woo!

572
00:25:26.460 --> 00:25:27.400
Well, you know—

573
00:25:27.400 --> 00:25:27.680
Hold on.

574
00:25:27.740 --> 00:25:28.360
What is your number?

575
00:25:28.520 --> 00:25:29.080
I have to know.

576
00:25:29.260 --> 00:25:30.040
You were close.

577
00:25:30.340 --> 00:25:31.160
I was?

578
00:25:31.320 --> 00:25:31.700
******.

579
00:25:34.320 --> 00:25:35.020
You got the area code.

580
00:25:35.020 --> 00:25:36.440
Oh, I got the area code.

581
00:25:36.540 --> 00:25:37.480
I mean, big whoop.

582
00:25:37.700 --> 00:25:39.460
What number was I calling?

583
00:25:39.640 --> 00:25:39.980
Oh.

584
00:25:40.500 --> 00:25:41.300
It's my accountant.

585
00:25:42.420 --> 00:25:42.860
Hey!

586
00:25:43.400 --> 00:25:46.650
I'm bad.

587
00:25:47.350 --> 00:25:48.190
That is hilarious.

588
00:25:48.190 --> 00:25:49.730
That's my accountant's phone number.

589
00:25:50.050 --> 00:25:51.210
I mean, listen.

590
00:25:51.470 --> 00:25:51.910
You know what?

591
00:25:51.910 --> 00:25:53.970
Listen, I have to call that number a lot.

592
00:25:54.350 --> 00:25:57.030
Like, when I'm in a bind or I have a question.

593
00:25:57.030 --> 00:25:59.690
But technically, you're calling an accountant.

594
00:25:59.930 --> 00:26:00.850
I'm calling an accountant.

595
00:26:01.230 --> 00:26:05.390
Well, when we reached out to Brent about this episode, he wanted to share with us about

596
00:26:05.390 --> 00:26:10.810
a joke he pitched for this scene and what he learned about writing for our show in this

597
00:26:10.810 --> 00:26:11.130
moment.

598
00:26:11.670 --> 00:26:16.750
So we're shooting the scene where Michael Scott is stranded at the gas station and he

599
00:26:16.750 --> 00:26:20.550
borrows the gas station attendant's phone to call the office.

600
00:26:20.550 --> 00:26:25.930
And we had written in the script that Michael realizes he doesn't know the number to the

601
00:26:25.930 --> 00:26:30.190
office because he always just presses the word office in the contacts on his phone.

602
00:26:30.610 --> 00:26:33.270
And Steve Carell was prepared to perform that.

603
00:26:33.730 --> 00:26:38.170
And right before we were going to shoot it, I pitched this extra joke.

604
00:26:38.310 --> 00:26:44.470
I said, what if Michael looks at the guy's phone and he sees contacts and he sees the

605
00:26:44.470 --> 00:26:51.550
guy has office in his contacts and Michael presses office and then the phone on the counter in

606
00:26:51.550 --> 00:26:56.010
front of the gas station attendant rings and the gas station attendant glares at him.

607
00:26:56.410 --> 00:26:58.710
And I felt very clever for pitching this.

608
00:26:58.890 --> 00:27:01.930
And Steve said, no, Michael is not that dumb.

609
00:27:02.130 --> 00:27:05.870
He knows that the number to his office is not on this guy's phone.

610
00:27:06.050 --> 00:27:09.030
So comedy fans, there's a great lesson here.

611
00:27:09.030 --> 00:27:16.910
The office is revered in great part because of the tone of realism, what we sometimes call

612
00:27:16.910 --> 00:27:17.670
naturalism.

613
00:27:17.810 --> 00:27:26.090
And the secret of naturalism is when you must choose between funny and real, err on the side

614
00:27:26.090 --> 00:27:26.570
of real.

615
00:27:26.790 --> 00:27:31.490
That's the great lesson that I learned from the great actors on The Office.

616
00:27:32.430 --> 00:27:36.310
Well, Angela, I have two more things to offer about this scene.

617
00:27:36.450 --> 00:27:36.730
Okay.

618
00:27:36.730 --> 00:27:40.410
I've got a gas station location tidbit.

619
00:27:41.030 --> 00:27:42.710
Not a breakdown, a tidbit.

620
00:27:42.850 --> 00:27:43.550
It's a tidbit.

621
00:27:43.690 --> 00:27:45.330
It's a breakdown slash tidbit.

622
00:27:45.470 --> 00:27:48.190
And then I also have a standards and practices name.

623
00:27:48.470 --> 00:27:50.110
Which one would you like first?

624
00:27:50.270 --> 00:27:51.770
Standards and practices, please.

625
00:27:52.150 --> 00:27:52.590
All right.

626
00:27:52.770 --> 00:27:54.190
Fan question from Corey E.

627
00:27:54.310 --> 00:27:55.170
in Denver, Colorado.

628
00:27:55.530 --> 00:28:00.590
In re-watching this episode, I noticed that when Michael goes into the gas station, over

629
00:28:00.590 --> 00:28:04.730
his shoulder, there is a grasshopper lawn care sign behind him.

630
00:28:04.730 --> 00:28:08.570
That is my friend's Pennsylvania-based lawn care company.

631
00:28:08.650 --> 00:28:12.610
I asked her about it, and she said, you guys did a prop drop.

632
00:28:12.930 --> 00:28:13.470
Oh.

633
00:28:13.790 --> 00:28:14.710
This is true.

634
00:28:15.170 --> 00:28:18.550
Phil Shea got this actual sign from a local company.

635
00:28:18.590 --> 00:28:23.610
He had it put up in the background of this scene to give it a kind of local credibility.

636
00:28:23.610 --> 00:28:30.750
However, Randy said that originally the sign had the real phone number for the business.

637
00:28:30.990 --> 00:28:36.390
It was like 1-800-LAWN-CARE, I think, and it is on the sign.

638
00:28:36.710 --> 00:28:39.530
And standards and practices flagged it.

639
00:28:39.690 --> 00:28:43.830
They said this is not a cleared phone number, and you're going to have to digitally remove

640
00:28:43.830 --> 00:28:44.830
it in post-production.

641
00:28:45.190 --> 00:28:47.390
So much sass from them, always.

642
00:28:47.570 --> 00:28:47.990
I know.

643
00:28:48.130 --> 00:28:51.930
But at least this time, they didn't have to type any curse words.

644
00:28:52.190 --> 00:28:52.290
Mm-mm.

645
00:28:52.650 --> 00:28:53.970
I wonder if they were bummed.

646
00:28:54.270 --> 00:28:57.110
I wonder if they were like, oh, just a sign.

647
00:28:57.270 --> 00:29:03.010
Actually, I think that their email said, please digitally remove the f***ing phone number

648
00:29:03.010 --> 00:29:03.850
from the sign.

649
00:29:03.850 --> 00:29:05.090
It is not cleared.

650
00:29:05.270 --> 00:29:06.750
As previously stated.

651
00:29:06.750 --> 00:29:07.510
Exactly.

652
00:29:08.450 --> 00:29:10.670
Please lose the f***ing phone number.

653
00:29:11.330 --> 00:29:13.930
Here is your location tidbit.

654
00:29:14.600 --> 00:29:18.110
We shot this at Civic Center Gas Station in Minimart.

655
00:29:18.290 --> 00:29:19.290
It's in Van Nuys.

656
00:29:19.690 --> 00:29:24.450
It is now closed, but their Yelp review page is still up.

657
00:29:24.670 --> 00:29:30.830
And it had one four-star review and three one-star reviews.

658
00:29:31.450 --> 00:29:32.850
Were they up the bathroom?

659
00:29:33.130 --> 00:29:35.490
Here is one of the one-star reviews.

660
00:29:35.690 --> 00:29:35.990
Okay.

661
00:29:36.550 --> 00:29:42.250
Quote, bathroom gets seven out of ten, mostly for a quiet, enjoyable time.

662
00:29:42.730 --> 00:29:43.290
What?

663
00:29:43.470 --> 00:29:44.770
What are you doing getting there?

664
00:29:45.450 --> 00:29:49.750
My friend was banging on the door to get my attention, and I couldn't hear his thing.

665
00:29:49.770 --> 00:29:50.070
Oh, come on.

666
00:29:50.250 --> 00:29:50.950
Come on.

667
00:29:50.990 --> 00:29:52.670
Made my experience peaceful.

668
00:29:52.990 --> 00:29:55.530
The cashier is another story, though.

669
00:29:56.030 --> 00:30:02.530
Not only was he rude, suggested my friend abandoned me there, my best friend, but when I

670
00:30:02.530 --> 00:30:07.070
needed to use his phone, he didn't have any of my numbers on speed dial for me to call

671
00:30:07.070 --> 00:30:07.590
a ride.

672
00:30:08.170 --> 00:30:11.970
Turns out my friend did leave me something about a baby emergency.

673
00:30:12.730 --> 00:30:13.910
Who wrote that?

674
00:30:14.350 --> 00:30:16.930
Was it one of our writers, or was that a fan of the show?

675
00:30:17.170 --> 00:30:18.510
No, it was just a fan of the show.

676
00:30:18.650 --> 00:30:19.590
That's brilliant.

677
00:30:19.850 --> 00:30:20.570
Brilliant, right?

678
00:30:20.790 --> 00:30:21.150
Yes.

679
00:30:21.310 --> 00:30:22.950
I really liked that little tidbit.

680
00:30:22.950 --> 00:30:23.490
Oh, my gosh.

681
00:30:23.690 --> 00:30:24.390
That's awesome.

682
00:30:25.130 --> 00:30:27.610
Well, Michael's going to have a talking head where he says, you know what?

683
00:30:27.670 --> 00:30:28.190
This is good.

684
00:30:28.370 --> 00:30:32.630
I'm actually going to take this opportunity to go walkabout, and no cameras.

685
00:30:32.790 --> 00:30:35.010
He shoos the cameras away, which he never does.

686
00:30:35.710 --> 00:30:38.150
There was a candy bag alt for this talking head.

687
00:30:38.150 --> 00:30:39.210
Oh, please.

688
00:30:40.070 --> 00:30:41.650
This is what Michael says.

689
00:30:42.030 --> 00:30:45.390
This was not the day for one of your prank-a-roonies, Jim.

690
00:30:45.630 --> 00:30:47.290
I would have loved this yesterday.

691
00:30:47.290 --> 00:30:49.470
I would have laughed my butt off.

692
00:30:49.590 --> 00:30:50.390
But you know what?

693
00:30:50.570 --> 00:30:52.510
This is a blessing in the skies.

694
00:30:52.990 --> 00:30:54.150
I'm going walkabout.

695
00:30:54.930 --> 00:30:56.150
Blessing in the skies.

696
00:30:56.230 --> 00:30:57.690
A blessing in the skies.

697
00:30:57.810 --> 00:31:00.530
I really like it when Michael gets phrases wrong.

698
00:31:00.710 --> 00:31:02.630
I do, too, because I can relate.

699
00:31:02.990 --> 00:31:05.190
I might have said blessing in the skies.

700
00:31:06.710 --> 00:31:09.550
Back at the office, a search party is forming.

701
00:31:09.730 --> 00:31:14.490
It is going to consist of Holly, Aaron, and Dwight.

702
00:31:15.010 --> 00:31:19.690
Yes, Dwight is going to say that he can't drive, though, because he's got a car full of fox meat.

703
00:31:20.330 --> 00:31:23.970
I can't even tell you how many alts they had for that line.

704
00:31:24.110 --> 00:31:25.130
For why he can't drive?

705
00:31:25.450 --> 00:31:27.090
Like two pages of alts.

706
00:31:27.150 --> 00:31:28.150
I'm not even kidding.

707
00:31:28.310 --> 00:31:28.930
I don't doubt it.

708
00:31:28.930 --> 00:31:31.710
I'm just going to give you three, because there were so many.

709
00:31:31.850 --> 00:31:32.170
Okay.

710
00:31:32.510 --> 00:31:33.210
Here they were.

711
00:31:33.930 --> 00:31:34.750
You drive.

712
00:31:34.750 --> 00:31:36.430
I have a car full of scarecrow heads.

713
00:31:37.590 --> 00:31:38.910
You laugh out loud.

714
00:31:40.050 --> 00:31:40.450
You drive.

715
00:31:40.690 --> 00:31:41.830
I have a car full of pennies.

716
00:31:42.090 --> 00:31:44.710
Stupid bank only lets me convert $10,000 a day.

717
00:31:45.430 --> 00:31:46.090
You drive.

718
00:31:46.290 --> 00:31:48.190
I have a car full of Christmas ornaments I'm returning.

719
00:31:48.470 --> 00:31:50.270
30 days, and I have the receipts.

720
00:31:51.150 --> 00:31:52.830
There were two pages of these.

721
00:31:53.510 --> 00:31:54.470
I want more.

722
00:31:54.630 --> 00:31:55.610
I wish there were more.

723
00:31:55.770 --> 00:31:56.390
There were.

724
00:31:56.550 --> 00:31:59.470
I just didn't know if I would bore you guys if I just read a ton of them.

725
00:31:59.590 --> 00:32:00.570
They're very funny.

726
00:32:01.250 --> 00:32:03.190
Well, listen, I think we should take a break.

727
00:32:03.610 --> 00:32:06.150
When we come back, we're going to learn all about sticky quips.

728
00:32:06.470 --> 00:32:07.430
Oh, yes.

729
00:32:07.630 --> 00:32:10.490
And we're going to see who has the best instincts for finding Michael.

730
00:32:19.200 --> 00:32:21.840
We're in the kitchen at eight minutes, five seconds.

731
00:32:22.100 --> 00:32:24.060
We get a nice shot of Pam's doodle.

732
00:32:24.680 --> 00:32:29.820
Randy shared with us that at this time, Henry Sane, our graphics designer, had left our show

733
00:32:29.820 --> 00:32:30.740
to direct a feature.

734
00:32:30.740 --> 00:32:32.360
We all loved Henry.

735
00:32:32.560 --> 00:32:33.380
He was amazing.

736
00:32:33.640 --> 00:32:38.500
But for this episode, we added a new member to our art department, Michael Bayeuth.

737
00:32:38.640 --> 00:32:41.240
And he would be our graphic artist for this episode.

738
00:32:41.380 --> 00:32:43.440
So that is who designed this doodle.

739
00:32:44.080 --> 00:32:50.380
Pam is very tickled because she notices that people have added captions to the bottom of

740
00:32:50.380 --> 00:32:50.980
her doodle.

741
00:32:51.100 --> 00:32:51.420
Yeah.

742
00:32:51.620 --> 00:32:52.560
And they're quite funny.

743
00:32:52.560 --> 00:32:57.200
So she's actually going to walk into the bullpen and say, guys, these are fun.

744
00:32:57.740 --> 00:32:58.060
Mm-hmm.

745
00:32:58.480 --> 00:33:02.180
This starts a whole caption contest runner for the episode.

746
00:33:02.180 --> 00:33:04.380
And everyone really wants to get involved.

747
00:33:04.560 --> 00:33:05.680
Especially Daryl.

748
00:33:06.020 --> 00:33:06.380
Yeah.

749
00:33:06.380 --> 00:33:11.180
Apparently, Daryl writes his own captions for Family Circus, and his daughter, like, cracks

750
00:33:11.180 --> 00:33:11.420
up.

751
00:33:11.500 --> 00:33:12.400
He's really good at it.

752
00:33:12.760 --> 00:33:15.440
I did a little mini deep dive on caption contests.

753
00:33:15.700 --> 00:33:16.980
Oh, I knew you would.

754
00:33:17.260 --> 00:33:23.540
So first of all, this episode of The Office appears on the Wikipedia page about caption

755
00:33:23.540 --> 00:33:24.180
contests.

756
00:33:24.240 --> 00:33:24.740
Does it really?

757
00:33:24.740 --> 00:33:26.320
And the history of caption contests.

758
00:33:26.420 --> 00:33:26.760
Yes.

759
00:33:27.540 --> 00:33:33.620
So according to this Wikipedia page, the first printed caption contest seems to have appeared

760
00:33:33.620 --> 00:33:38.280
in Life magazine, which ran from 1883 to 1936.

761
00:33:38.980 --> 00:33:41.140
But this was fascinating to me.

762
00:33:41.420 --> 00:33:44.200
They were not called caption contests.

763
00:33:44.580 --> 00:33:49.980
They were called picture title contests or picture headline contests.

764
00:33:50.660 --> 00:33:51.920
And here's why.

765
00:33:52.120 --> 00:33:52.420
Why?

766
00:33:52.760 --> 00:33:55.220
The word caption didn't exiSt.

767
00:33:55.460 --> 00:33:55.980
Come on.

768
00:33:56.120 --> 00:33:56.940
It didn't.

769
00:33:57.040 --> 00:33:58.380
Who invented the word caption?

770
00:33:58.660 --> 00:34:02.420
I don't know, but it did not show up in the English language until the first half of

771
00:34:02.420 --> 00:34:03.280
the 20th century.

772
00:34:03.620 --> 00:34:04.000
Huh.

773
00:34:04.400 --> 00:34:07.720
Now you go on Instagram, people post a photo and say, caption this.

774
00:34:07.880 --> 00:34:08.140
Yeah.

775
00:34:08.360 --> 00:34:10.540
You better be careful when you write that sometimes.

776
00:34:11.840 --> 00:34:13.100
Well, that is true.

777
00:34:13.920 --> 00:34:16.500
And I've got a little fun fact as well.

778
00:34:16.660 --> 00:34:16.880
Okay.

779
00:34:17.620 --> 00:34:21.520
OfficeTally.com ran a caption contest after this episode aired.

780
00:34:21.700 --> 00:34:25.920
They put up the drawing of the Sabre printer, and people could submit captions.

781
00:34:26.480 --> 00:34:28.480
And here were a couple that I thought were fun.

782
00:34:28.600 --> 00:34:29.420
Was there a winner?

783
00:34:29.720 --> 00:34:30.420
There was.

784
00:34:30.420 --> 00:34:32.900
And the winner got a prize package.

785
00:34:32.900 --> 00:34:40.120
They got a DVD, The Office Overtime, a Creed Thoughts shirt, and a Dunder Mifflin coffee

786
00:34:40.120 --> 00:34:40.500
tumbler.

787
00:34:40.520 --> 00:34:41.240
So fun.

788
00:34:41.400 --> 00:34:41.600
Right?

789
00:34:41.800 --> 00:34:42.560
Let's hear some.

790
00:34:42.840 --> 00:34:43.080
Okay.

791
00:34:43.220 --> 00:34:50.120
So the contest page is down, but some people had also submitted their captions in the comments,

792
00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:51.300
which are still up.

793
00:34:52.100 --> 00:34:56.480
Brick wrote, quote, I work so hard so my son can become a scanner.

794
00:34:57.420 --> 00:34:58.400
That was one.

795
00:34:58.400 --> 00:35:02.620
And then Jordan went with, hey, look over here.

796
00:35:02.620 --> 00:35:04.860
I'm just about as white and square as Gabe.

797
00:35:05.600 --> 00:35:09.260
So he really went in the direction of the episode.

798
00:35:10.960 --> 00:35:13.220
Dwight, Holly, and Erin are searching for Michael.

799
00:35:13.380 --> 00:35:14.780
They start with the gas station.

800
00:35:14.980 --> 00:35:18.520
They ask the gas station attendant which way Michael went.

801
00:35:18.760 --> 00:35:22.520
And as it turns out, it's exactly where Holly would have gone.

802
00:35:22.700 --> 00:35:27.000
She had predicted that he might have gone in the direction of the bakery just for the smell.

803
00:35:27.560 --> 00:35:29.040
And that's the way he went.

804
00:35:30.020 --> 00:35:30.180
Yeah.

805
00:35:30.600 --> 00:35:37.720
At 9 minutes and 37 seconds, you see this sign for a bakery, the thing that Holly sees.

806
00:35:37.860 --> 00:35:38.760
That was a fake bakery.

807
00:35:39.000 --> 00:35:39.660
It doesn't exiSt.

808
00:35:39.800 --> 00:35:40.640
We made that sign.

809
00:35:41.080 --> 00:35:46.600
And I guess past the bakery, Michael's going to wander into a pet store where he's going

810
00:35:46.600 --> 00:35:48.220
to start talking to the animals.

811
00:35:48.900 --> 00:35:53.180
This was a real pet store on Victory Boulevard in Burbank, California.

812
00:35:53.180 --> 00:35:57.160
It was filled with animals, but we didn't use their animals.

813
00:35:57.160 --> 00:35:59.040
We brought in our own animals.

814
00:35:59.240 --> 00:36:02.240
They were provided by Bob Dunn's Animal Services.

815
00:36:02.740 --> 00:36:04.600
You know, we used them all the time.

816
00:36:04.800 --> 00:36:06.740
Our head trainer was Denise Sanders.

817
00:36:07.300 --> 00:36:10.780
Randy sent us the photos of the puppies that they caSt.

818
00:36:11.080 --> 00:36:12.040
It's so cute.

819
00:36:12.040 --> 00:36:12.920
I'll put it in stories.

820
00:36:13.080 --> 00:36:15.100
We have all the pictures of these little dogs.

821
00:36:15.420 --> 00:36:16.580
That's the job I want.

822
00:36:16.700 --> 00:36:22.520
I want the job where I look through the puppy pictures and I pick puppies for a day of work.

823
00:36:23.180 --> 00:36:27.860
We don't know the names of the tiny golden retrievers, but the Yorkie was played by Amos.

824
