Michael talks to a macaw who was named Hook.

825
00:36:31.580 --> 00:36:32.980
He talks to a snake.

826
00:36:33.100 --> 00:36:34.080
We don't know the snake's name.

827
00:36:34.200 --> 00:36:34.660
Sorry, guys.

828
00:36:35.080 --> 00:36:39.060
And we used two puppies to play the, quote, licking puppy.

829
00:36:39.280 --> 00:36:40.400
They were very cute.

830
00:36:40.700 --> 00:36:43.080
You know, we got a fan question from Natasha C.

831
00:36:43.180 --> 00:36:43.980
in Dallas, Texas.

832
00:36:43.980 --> 00:36:48.160
When Michael was in the pet store holding the puppy, the puppy licks his nose.

833
00:36:48.440 --> 00:36:53.360
Was the puppy trained to do this or was this just perfect timing and improv on Michael's part?

834
00:36:53.820 --> 00:36:55.620
I'll tell you what, Natasha.

835
00:36:56.000 --> 00:36:58.400
All of the little pet store vignettes were scripted.

836
00:36:58.540 --> 00:37:00.360
They sound so off the cuff.

837
00:37:00.540 --> 00:37:06.440
But everything, the things he says to the snake, the thing he says to the macaw, all scripted,

838
00:37:06.440 --> 00:37:10.020
included, including the part where the puppy was supposed to lick his nose.

839
00:37:10.220 --> 00:37:14.780
The puppy licks his nose exactly in the spot it's supposed to.

840
00:37:14.860 --> 00:37:16.260
I don't know how they did it.

841
00:37:16.260 --> 00:37:17.280
How did they pull that off?

842
00:37:17.280 --> 00:37:19.760
I don't know because it's not supposed to lick him at firSt.

843
00:37:19.760 --> 00:37:21.260
It's supposed to lick him in the middle.

844
00:37:21.660 --> 00:37:22.620
All scripted.

845
00:37:22.960 --> 00:37:28.140
And Steve did such a great job of making that just feel like 100% spontaneous.

846
00:37:28.340 --> 00:37:28.620
I know.

847
00:37:28.760 --> 00:37:29.180
All of it.

848
00:37:29.520 --> 00:37:33.300
Back at the office, Pam was like, well, if I knew you guys loved caption contests,

849
00:37:33.300 --> 00:37:35.860
I would have drawn something different that you could really have fun with.

850
00:37:35.900 --> 00:37:36.920
And so she does.

851
00:37:37.220 --> 00:37:41.660
She draws two large dogs on an island with two palm trees.

852
00:37:42.000 --> 00:37:46.440
But before anybody can give their captions, Gabe kind of shuts it down.

853
00:37:46.740 --> 00:37:48.480
Yeah, he's a total fun stopper.

854
00:37:48.960 --> 00:37:52.560
He says they can do it if they can agree on some ground rules.

855
00:37:52.760 --> 00:37:55.060
Number one, no insulting the company.

856
00:37:55.360 --> 00:37:57.640
Number two, no pop culture references.

857
00:37:58.320 --> 00:38:02.860
And finally, they have to write their captions on Dunder Mifflin's sabers.

858
00:38:03.300 --> 00:38:04.360
Sticky quips.

859
00:38:04.660 --> 00:38:05.180
Yes.

860
00:38:05.460 --> 00:38:06.840
And then Gabe has a talking head.

861
00:38:07.000 --> 00:38:08.640
He says, sticky quips are fun.

862
00:38:08.820 --> 00:38:09.340
They're safe.

863
00:38:09.520 --> 00:38:10.040
They're handy.

864
00:38:10.320 --> 00:38:14.580
I like to use sticky quips as regular post-it notes when I'm in a fun mood.

865
00:38:14.820 --> 00:38:15.800
Not every day.

866
00:38:16.240 --> 00:38:19.560
There was an alt for this talking head where Gabe simply just said,

867
00:38:19.700 --> 00:38:21.920
I don't have a lot of responsibility here.

868
00:38:22.360 --> 00:38:24.600
Oh, wow.

869
00:38:26.180 --> 00:38:28.520
We had a fan question from Joshua P.

870
00:38:28.800 --> 00:38:29.500
in Cincinnati.

871
00:38:29.500 --> 00:38:35.180
Did sticky quips already exist out there somewhere or did Phil Shea have to find a way to create them?

872
00:38:35.500 --> 00:38:37.320
Well, Joshua, they did not exiSt.

873
00:38:37.900 --> 00:38:39.920
Let's give you a visual of a sticky quip.

874
00:38:40.060 --> 00:38:43.680
It's basically a post-it note, but it's shaped like a speech bubble.

875
00:38:43.680 --> 00:38:44.660
Like an idea bubble.

876
00:38:44.820 --> 00:38:45.020
Yeah.

877
00:38:45.300 --> 00:38:50.680
And Phil Shea had to design them, and he used Earl Hay Press to manufacture and package them.

878
00:38:50.880 --> 00:38:53.520
But I found what I'm calling a Reddit catch.

879
00:38:53.660 --> 00:38:53.920
Oh.

880
00:38:54.360 --> 00:38:58.160
When I googled sticky quips, I found this catch on Reddit.

881
00:38:58.480 --> 00:38:59.680
It's pretty fantastic.

882
00:38:59.900 --> 00:39:00.500
Let's hear it.

883
00:39:00.500 --> 00:39:08.960
It says, at 15 minutes and 32 seconds, in the pilot episode of The Office, Jim has a doodle taped to his computer monitor.

884
00:39:08.960 --> 00:39:12.460
It's while he's eating some Jell-O.

885
00:39:12.680 --> 00:39:12.920
Okay.

886
00:39:13.380 --> 00:39:19.640
And there are four mini sticky notes with word bubbles drawn on them with captions inside.

887
00:39:19.960 --> 00:39:28.320
So someone has captioned this doodle, or four people have captioned this doodle, using sort of the original sticky quip.

888
00:39:28.540 --> 00:39:28.900
Huh.

889
00:39:29.140 --> 00:39:30.060
It was kind of cool.

890
00:39:30.060 --> 00:39:30.520
Yeah.

891
00:39:31.340 --> 00:39:35.440
Well, we asked Brent about the name Sticky Quips, and this is what he had to say.

892
00:39:35.620 --> 00:39:40.660
He said, that's just one of those two-word phrases that comedy writers love.

893
00:39:40.780 --> 00:39:47.740
It's got multiple K sounds, plus a T and a P, five plosives in two words.

894
00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:49.560
That's comedy power, people.

895
00:39:50.200 --> 00:39:54.720
Angela, could you write a good caption for a picture?

896
00:39:54.900 --> 00:39:55.700
Because I could not.

897
00:39:55.860 --> 00:39:57.140
I just am not that clever.

898
00:39:57.460 --> 00:39:58.660
I do enjoy it.

899
00:39:58.880 --> 00:39:59.200
Really?

900
00:39:59.200 --> 00:39:59.560
I like it.

901
00:39:59.660 --> 00:39:59.860
Yes.

902
00:39:59.860 --> 00:40:01.580
And they often do it at school.

903
00:40:01.760 --> 00:40:03.460
You know, our son is in sixth grade.

904
00:40:03.640 --> 00:40:06.160
They do a caption contest, and he won one week.

905
00:40:06.340 --> 00:40:06.660
Really?

906
00:40:06.840 --> 00:40:07.060
Mm-hmm.

907
00:40:07.220 --> 00:40:08.140
Oh, my gosh.

908
00:40:08.380 --> 00:40:09.920
I'm very impressed by that.

909
00:40:10.100 --> 00:40:14.460
I think maybe that is the same thing that would hold me back as an improviser.

910
00:40:14.580 --> 00:40:16.120
I just get in my head about it.

911
00:40:16.180 --> 00:40:16.400
Mm-hmm.

912
00:40:16.400 --> 00:40:19.000
And I can't, it doesn't flow for me.

913
00:40:19.640 --> 00:40:21.460
Oh, I love a good caption conteSt.

914
00:40:21.560 --> 00:40:22.860
Well, you're an improv person.

915
00:40:23.000 --> 00:40:24.920
I feel like that makes so much sense.

